module bindings.javascript.fastify_encapsulated_middleware_reprefix;

// A Fastify plugin that offers connect-style middleware has to decide what a
// middleware path means, and the documented answer is that it is relative to
// the scope registering it: `instance.prefix` is that scope's route prefix, so
// `use('/admin', fn)` inside a plugin mounted at `/api` guards `/api/admin`.
// A plugin implements that by prepending `this.prefix` to the path it is given
// and storing the absolute result, which is what its middleware engine needs.
//
// Encapsulation is the other half. A child plugin gets a fresh context that
// inherits nothing by reference -- decorators do not cross the boundary -- so
// the plugin subscribes to `onRegister`, documented as firing when a new
// encapsulation context is created, re-decorates `use` on the child, and
// re-registers whatever the parent had so the child's routes stay covered.
//
// Those two halves do not compose. The stored paths are already absolute,
// because the prefixing registration function is what stored them; the child's
// `prefix` is longer than the parent's, so handing them back to that same
// function prepends the child's prefix a second time. A middleware registered
// at `/admin` becomes `/admin/admin` inside a child mounted at `/admin`, which
// matches nothing the child serves. The middleware does not error and does not
// warn -- it silently stops running, and if it was the authentication,
// authorization or rate-limiting middleware, every route in that child scope
// and in each of its nested scopes is served unguarded.
//
// The correct propagation hands the stored pair to the middleware engine
// underneath the instance, which takes an absolute path and applies no prefix
// of its own, and keeps the plugin's own bookkeeping in sync by hand.
//
// So the binding reads four facts out of the plugin function, which is where
// all of them are visible together because the registration function and the
// hook are nested declarations inside it:
//
//   * the registration function is prefix-relative -- it reads `this.prefix`;
//   * the plugin installs the `onRegister` encapsulation hook;
//   * something re-decorates a context, which is the boundary-crossing that
//     hook exists for;
//   * and the prefix-prepending registration function is called on that
//     context: `instance.use(...)`.
//
// The fourth is the defect and it is stated positively, as the call the
// vulnerable plugin makes, rather than as the absence of whatever a fix put in
// its place -- the two fixes of this shape reach the engine by different routes
// (a symbol-keyed subscript in one, a decorated `express` app in the other) and
// the binding names neither.
//
// Every conjunct is an exact token rather than a substring, because `contains`
// would read `instance.user` and `instance.useCache` as `instance.use` and
// `this.prefixLength` as `this.prefix`. `instance` is how Fastify's own
// onRegister documentation spells the encapsulation context it hands the hook;
// a plugin that names that parameter something else is not matched, which is
// the price of the exact form.
binding fastifyEncapsulatedMiddlewareReprefix {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.selector == "this.prefix"
    and node.context.literal == "onRegister"
    and node.context.call == "decorate"
    and node.context.callPath == "instance.use"
  emit issue code.EncapsulatedMiddlewarePathReprefixed at node
}
